// 090 — Compile-time vs runtime branching
// Two different questions, two different tools:
//
//   * "Which code path do I want the *compiler* to even emit?"
//     C answers with #ifdef. Rust with cfg!. AiLang answers by
//     organizing files: per-OS code lives in OS-specific libraries
//     (Library.OS.Linux.* vs .Haiku.*) and the build picks one.
//     There is no preprocessor.
//
//   * "Which code path do I want to run *this time*?"
//     That's IfCondition, Branch, or Fork — ordinary runtime control.
//
// Conflating the two (one keyword that's sometimes compile-time and
// sometimes runtime, like C++ `if constexpr`) is a known source of
// "the build worked but the binary does the wrong thing." AiLang
// keeps them apart so the answer to "where is this decided?" never
// requires reading the build system.
SubRoutine.Main {
    // Plain runtime branch — decided at execution time.
    n = 7
    IfCondition GreaterThan(n, 5) ThenBlock: {
        PrintMessage("runtime: n is large\n")
    } ElseBlock: {
        PrintMessage("runtime: n is small\n")
    }

    // The "compile-time" version of the same question is structural,
    // not syntactic: a different .ailang file is included by the build,
    // so this entire SubRoutine has different bytes per target.
    PrintMessage("compile-time decisions live in the build, not the source\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
